{{Politics of Okaiken}}

The '''Ministry of Land, Infrastructure, Transport and Tourism''' ({{Nihongo|国土交通省|Kokudō-kōtsū-shō}}), commonly abbreviated '''MLIT''', is a [[Cabinet of Okaiken|cabinet-level ministry]] of the [[Government of Okaiken]] responsible for the development and management of the nation's [[transport infrastructure]], [[land use]] planning, [[public works]], [[maritime transport|maritime affairs]], [[aviation policy|aviation]], and [[tourism]] promotion. It is one of the largest and most consequential ministries in the Sanese government, with a budget of approximately {{currency|128 billion|SEN}} in fiscal year 2025 — roughly 8% of total government expenditure — and approximately 67,400 employees across its headquarters, regional bureaus, and affiliated agencies.

The ministry oversees the entirety of Okaiken's transport network, including the [[Shinkansen (Okaiken)|Shinkansen]] high-speed rail system, the [[Oka-Pass]] ({{Nihongo|オカパス|Okapasu}}) automated expressway system, the country's ports and harbours, and its civil aviation infrastructure. It also administers the [[Okaiken Tourism Agency]], the [[Land Infrastructure Management and Construction Industry Agency (Okaiken)|Land Infrastructure Management Agency]], and — since May 2026 — the [[Yamamoto Hina Safety Commission]], an independent rail safety body with unprecedented authority to ground rail services without ministerial approval.

The current Minister of Land, Infrastructure, Transport and Tourism is [[Seiran Mochizuki]] of the [[Okaiken Socialist Party|Okaiken Socialist Party (OSP)]], who assumed office on 20 May 2026 as part of the [[Ikazaki Cabinet]]. Mochizuki, a former [[Okaiken Transport Workers Union|OTWU]] organiser and MR for [[Okami Metropolis|Okami]] 6th, has direct oversight of the Yamamoto Hina Safety Commission. Her predecessor was [[Yasushi Kaneko]] of the [[People's Party of Okaiken|People's Party of Okaiken (PPO)]], who served from August 2021 to May 2026 under the [[Honda-Nakayama Cabinet]].

==History==

===Predecessor ministries (1870–2001)===

The institutional origins of the MLIT lie in several imperial-era ministries and agencies. The {{Nihongo|Ministry of Construction|建設省|Kensetsu-shō}}, established in 1948 during the post-war reconstruction, was responsible for public works, river management, and urban infrastructure. The {{Nihongo|Ministry of Transport|運輸省|Un'yu-shō}}, established in 1870 as the {{Nihongo|Ministry of Railways|鉄道省|Tetsudō-shō}} and renamed in 1943, oversaw rail, maritime, and aviation transport. The {{Nihongo|National Land Agency|国土庁|Kokudo-chō}}, established in 1974, was responsible for national land-use planning and regional development. The {{Nihongo|Hokkaidō Development Agency|北海道開発庁|Hokkaidō Kaihatsu-chō}} administered the development of Okaiken's northernmost main island.

Throughout the post-war period, the Ministry of Construction was one of the most powerful ministries in the Sanese bureaucracy, controlling the vast public works budget that underpinned the [[Sanese Industrial Miracle]]. The so-called {{Nihongo|"construction state"|土建国家|Doken Kokka}} — a system of political patronage linking construction firms, bureaucrats, and rural politicians — defined much of Okaiken's infrastructure development from the 1960s through the 1990s. The Ministry of Transport, meanwhile, oversaw the expansion of the Shinkansen network and the privatisation of the Sanese National Railways in 1987, which created the three [[Okaiken Railways Group]] enterprises that dominate the sector to this day.

===2001 Central Government Reform===
{{Main|2001 Central Government Reform (Okaiken)}}
The [[2001 Central Government Reform (Okaiken)|2001 Central Government Reform]] ({{Nihongo|中央省庁再編|Chūō Shōchō Saihen}}) merged the Ministry of Construction, the Ministry of Transport, the National Land Agency, and the Hokkaidō Development Agency into a single entity — the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure, Transport and Tourism. The merger, which took effect on 6 January 2001, was the most significant bureaucratic reorganisation in Okaiken since the post-war occupation, reducing the number of central ministries from 22 to 13.

The stated rationale for the merger was to improve policy coordination across the interconnected domains of land use, infrastructure development, and transport, and to reduce bureaucratic silos that had led to inefficiencies and inter-ministerial conflict. Critics, however, argued that the merger created an unwieldy "super-ministry" with an excessively broad mandate, and that the dominant culture of the former Construction Ministry — particularly its emphasis on public works spending — subsumed the transport and land-use portfolios.

===Post-merger era (2001–present)===

The merged ministry's first decade was dominated by the challenge of managing Okaiken's maturing infrastructure stock while reducing the public works budget, which had been a key driver of the nation's mounting fiscal deficit. The {{Nihongo|"Doken Reform"|土建改革|Doken Kaikaku}} of 2003–2008, initiated under the [[Kawaguchi administration]], sought to curb the construction state's excesses by introducing competitive bidding for public works contracts and reducing the number of amakudari ({{Nihongo|天下り|amakudari|"descent from heaven"}}) placements of retired bureaucrats into construction industry positions.

The [[Aoyagi administration (Okaiken)|Aoyagi administration]] (2014–2021) introduced significant reforms to the MLIT's approach to infrastructure investment, shifting emphasis from new construction to maintenance and renewal of existing assets. The {{Nihongo|"Infrastructure Lifecycle Management Plan"|インフラライフサイクル管理計画|Infura Raifusaikuru Kanri Keikaku}}, published in 2016, was the first comprehensive assessment of Okaiken's ageing infrastructure stock and projected that approximately {{currency|40 trillion|SEN}} in maintenance spending would be required over the subsequent 30 years.

The [[Honda Shigetsuma|Honda]] administration (2021–2026) reversed several Aoyagi-era policies, accelerating the deployment of one-man operation ({{Nihongo|ワンマン運転|Wanman Unten}}) on conventional rail lines and pursuing the privatisation of maintenance functions through outsourcing to private contractors. These policies would later become central to the controversies surrounding the [[Nakayama Station dragging incident]] and the subsequent formation of the [[Yamamoto Hina Safety Commission]].

==Organization==

The MLIT is organised into internal bureaus, regional bureaus, and affiliated agencies. As of 2026, the ministry employs approximately 67,400 personnel across all its divisions, making it the second-largest ministry by headcount after the [[Ministry of Health, Labour and Welfare (Okaiken)|Ministry of Health, Labour and Welfare]].

===Internal bureaus===

;Minister's Secretariat ({{Nihongo|大臣官房|Daijin Kanbō}})
:The Minister's Secretariat coordinates the ministry's overall operations, manages personnel, handles public relations, and serves as the primary interface between the ministry and the Cabinet. It also oversees the ministry's internal audit functions, information security protocols, and the coordination of cross-bureau policy initiatives.

;City Bureau ({{Nihongo|都市局|Toshi-kyoku}})
:The City Bureau is responsible for urban planning, city development, and the management of the {{Nihongo|"Okami magnet"|岡見磁器|Okami Jiki}} population concentration problem — the accelerating migration of population and economic activity to the Okami metropolitan area, which has created severe infrastructure strain and regional imbalances. The bureau administers the [[Decentralised National Land Formation Plan]] ({{Nihongo|分散型国土形成計画|Bunsan-gata Kokudo Keisei Keikaku}}) and oversees urban renewal projects in major cities.

;Road Bureau ({{Nihongo|道路局|Dōro-kyoku}})
:The Road Bureau administers the national expressway network, including the [[Oka-Pass]] ({{Nihongo|オカパス|Okapasu}}) automated toll collection system, which uses electronic toll collection (ETC) technology across all expressways. The bureau oversees the construction and maintenance of national highways, bridges, and tunnels, including the [[Minatojima Tunnel]] that carries the [[Ishijima Shinkansen]] under the [[Katsuran Strait]]. It also manages the {{Nihongo|Road Improvement Special Account|道路整備特別会計|Dōro Seibi Tokubetsu Kaikei}}, which funds expressway construction and maintenance.

;Railway Bureau ({{Nihongo|鉄道局|Tetsudō-kyoku}})
:The Railway Bureau oversees the regulation and development of Okaiken's rail network, including the [[Shinkansen (Okaiken)|Shinkansen]] high-speed rail system, the three [[Okaiken Railways Group]] enterprises ({{Nihongo|北岡鉄道|Kitaoka Tetsudō}}, {{Nihongo|東岡鉄道|Higaashioka Tetsudō}}, and {{Nihongo|西岡鉄道|Nishioka Tetsudō}}), and private rail operators such as [[Mitoyo Tetsudo]]. The bureau administers the [[Railway Business Act (Okaiken)|Railway Business Act]], licenses rail operators, and — since May 2026 — coordinates with the [[Yamamoto Hina Safety Commission]] on safety enforcement. The bureau also oversees the [[Cargo-Shinkansen]] ({{Nihongo|貨物新幹線|Kamotsu Shinkansen}}) high-speed freight rail lines.

;Maritime Bureau ({{Nihongo|海事局|Kaiji-kyoku}})
:The Maritime Bureau administers maritime transport policy, port management, and shipping regulation. It oversees Okaiken's major ports, including [[Aizawa Port]] — the country's largest maritime hub, which features [[Satori AI|Satori]]-controlled automated cranes — as well as the [[Okaiken Coast Guard]], which operates under the bureau's authority. The bureau also administers the [[Port Act (Okaiken)|Port Act]] and regulates the shipbuilding and shipping industries.

;Aviation Bureau ({{Nihongo|航空局|Kōkū-kyoku}})
:The Aviation Bureau is responsible for civil aviation policy, airport management, and air traffic control. It oversees Okaiken's major airports, including [[Okami International Airport]], and administers bilateral and multilateral aviation agreements. The bureau also coordinates with the [[International Civil Aviation Organization]] (ICAO) on safety standards and air navigation procedures.

;River Bureau ({{Nihongo|河川局|Kasen-kyoku}})
:The River Bureau manages the nation's river systems, flood control infrastructure, and dam construction and maintenance. It administers the {{Nihongo|River Act|河川法|Kasen Hō}} and oversees the management of approximately 10,800 rivers and 3,200 dams across Okaiken. The bureau also coordinates disaster preparedness and response for flood-related emergencies.

;Land and Water Bureau ({{Nihongo|土地・水資源局|Tochi/Mizushigen-kyoku}})
:The Land and Water Bureau is responsible for national land-use planning, water resource management, and the administration of the [[National Land Use Planning Act (Okaiken)|National Land Use Planning Act]]. It manages the national land survey and cadastral records, and coordinates the allocation of water rights for agricultural, industrial, and domestic use.

;Housing Bureau ({{Nihongo|住宅局|Jūtaku-kyoku}})
:The Housing Bureau administers the national housing policy, including the provision of public housing, the regulation of the private rental market, and the promotion of earthquake-resistant construction standards. It oversees the {{Nihongo|Housing Loan Support Agency|住宅金融支援機構|Jūtaku Kin'yū Shien Kikō}}, which provides government-backed mortgage financing.

===Regional bureaus===

The MLIT maintains eight regional development bureaus ({{Nihongo|地方整備局|Chihō Seibi-kyoku}}) that oversee infrastructure and construction projects in their respective regions, as well as the [[Okami Metropolitan Area Bureau]] ({{Nihongo|岡見圏整備局|Okami-ken Seibi-kyoku}}), which coordinates the complex infrastructure needs of the capital region. The regional bureaus employ approximately 42,000 of the ministry's total staff.

==Key agencies==

===Okaiken Tourism Agency===
{{Main|Okaiken Tourism Agency}}
The '''Okaiken Tourism Agency''' ({{Nihongo|沖軒観光庁|Okaiken Kankō-chō}}) is an external bureau of the MLIT responsible for the promotion of domestic and international tourism. Established in 2008 as part of the {{Nihongo|"Tourism Nation"|観光立国|Kankō Rikkoku}} initiative, the agency oversees the {{Nihongo|"Onsen Wellness Tourism"|温泉ウェルネスツーリズム|Onsen Werunesu Tsūrizumu}} programme, which generates approximately {{currency|210 billion|SEN}} annually and attracts 4.2 million international visitors to Okaiken's hot spring resorts. The agency also administers the [[Okaiken National Parks]] system and coordinates with local governments on tourism infrastructure development.

The agency's budget for FY 2025 was approximately {{currency|3.2 billion|SEN}}, of which approximately 60% was allocated to international marketing and promotion, 25% to domestic tourism infrastructure, and 15% to research and policy development.

===Land Infrastructure Management and Construction Industry Agency===
{{Main|Land Infrastructure Management and Construction Industry Agency (Okaiken)}}
The '''Land Infrastructure Management and Construction Industry Agency''' ({{Nihongo|土地・建設産業整備機構|Tochi/Kensetsu Sangyō Seibi Kikō}}) is an independent administrative institution under the MLIT that manages government-owned land, oversees the construction industry licensing system, and administers public works tenders. The agency was established in 2003 as part of the post-merger reorganisation and employs approximately 2,800 staff.

The agency has been at the centre of the [[Kizan prosecution]] controversy, as it was responsible for overseeing the private contractor whose maintenance failures contributed to the [[Nakayama Station dragging incident]].

===Okaiken Coast Guard===
{{Main|Okaiken Coast Guard}}
The '''Okaiken Coast Guard''' ({{Nihongo|沖軒海上保安庁|Okaiken Kaijō Hoan-chō}}) operates under the authority of the MLIT's Maritime Bureau and is responsible for maritime safety, search and rescue, maritime law enforcement, and the protection of Okaiken's territorial waters. The Coast Guard operates approximately 120 vessels and 40 aircraft, and employs approximately 12,500 personnel.

===Yamamoto Hina Safety Commission===
{{Main|Yamamoto Hina Safety Commission}}
The '''Yamamoto Hina Safety Commission''' ({{Nihongo|山本日奈安全委員会|Yamamoto Hina Anzen Iinkai}}) is an independent rail safety body established by executive order on 20 May 2026 as part of the [[Ikazaki Cabinet]]'s first day in office. Named after 17-year-old [[Yamamoto Hina]], who was killed in the [[Nakayama Station dragging incident]] on 1 March 2026, the Commission has the unprecedented authority to ground any rail service in Okaiken without ministerial approval if it determines that staffing levels or safety protocols are insufficient.

The Commission's creation was a core demand of the [[Okaiken Socialist Party|OSP]] during the coalition negotiations that formed the Ikazaki government. Transport Minister [[Seiran Mochizuki]] — herself a former [[Okaiken Transport Workers Union|OTWU]] organiser — has direct oversight of the Commission's implementation, though the Commission's independence from ministerial direction is enshrined in its founding executive order.

The Commission comprises seven members — three appointed by the Minister of Transport, two by the [[Diet of Okaiken|National Diet]], and two by the [[Supreme Court of Okaiken|Supreme Court]] — serving staggered five-year terms. Its first Chair is {{Nihongo|Noriko Shiomi|塩見法子|Shiomi Noriko}}, a former rail safety investigator and academic.

==Key policy areas==

===Rail transport===
{{Main|Rail transport in Okaiken}}
Okaiken's rail network is one of the most extensive and heavily used in the world, carrying approximately 24 million passengers daily across conventional and high-speed lines. The [[Shinkansen (Okaiken)|Shinkansen]] network connects all major metropolitan areas, with maximum operating speeds of 320 km/h on the newest lines. The three [[Okaiken Railways Group]] enterprises — {{Nihongo|Kitaoka Tetsudō|北岡鉄道}} (serving the northern and central regions), {{Nihongo|Higaashioka Tetsudō|東岡鉄道}} (serving the eastern regions), and {{Nihongo|Nishioka Tetsudō|西岡鉄道}} (serving the western regions) — operate the conventional rail network under licence from the MLIT. Private operators, most notably [[Mitoyo Tetsudo]], compete on regional and commuter lines.

The [[Cargo-Shinkansen]] ({{Nihongo|貨物新幹線|Kamotsu Shinkansen}}) programme, initiated in 2018, repurposes Shinkansen infrastructure for high-speed freight transport, enabling same-day delivery of high-value goods between major cities. The programme has been controversial, with critics arguing that the investment in freight Shinkansen has diverted resources from conventional freight rail and road logistics.

The MLIT's Railway Bureau administers the [[Railway Business Act (Okaiken)|Railway Business Act]], which governs the licensing, operation, and safety standards of all rail operators. Since May 2026, the Bureau has been required to coordinate with the [[Yamamoto Hina Safety Commission]] on all safety-related matters, and the Commission's grounding authority supersedes the Bureau's operational discretion.

===Roads and expressways===
The MLIT administers approximately 9,500 km of national expressways and 55,000 km of national highways. The [[Oka-Pass]] ({{Nihongo|オカパス|Okapasu}}) automated expressway system, introduced nationwide in 2015, uses electronic toll collection (ETC) technology to eliminate manual toll booths, reducing congestion and enabling variable toll pricing based on traffic conditions and time of day. The Oka-Pass system processes approximately 120 million transactions monthly.

The [[Minatojima Tunnel]] ({{Nihongo|湊島トンネル|Minatojima Tonneru}}), which carries the [[Ishijima Shinkansen]] under the [[Katsuran Strait]], is one of the MLIT's flagship infrastructure projects. Completed in 2022 at a cost of approximately {{currency|1.8 billion|SEN}}, the tunnel is the longest undersea rail tunnel in Okaiken and has significantly reduced travel times between the main island and the Ishijima region.

===Maritime transport===
Okaiken's maritime sector is centred on [[Aizawa Port]], the country's largest maritime hub, which handles approximately 35% of the nation's containerised cargo and 20% of its total trade volume by value. Aizawa Port's operations are notable for their extensive use of [[Satori AI|Satori]]-controlled automated cranes, which were introduced in 2023 as part of the port's modernisation programme. The Satori system manages crane operations, container stacking, and berth allocation, achieving a 40% improvement in throughput compared to manually operated systems.

The reliance on Satori-controlled automation at Aizawa Port became a significant concern following the [[Satori AI Blackout]] of May 2026, which caused a complete halt of crane operations for 72 hours and a backlog of approximately 15,000 containers. The incident prompted the MLIT to commission an independent review of AI dependency in critical port infrastructure, with findings expected by the end of 2026.

Other major ports include [[Sakai Port]], [[Hakata Port]], and [[Kiso Port]], which collectively handle the majority of Okaiken's remaining maritime trade.

===Aviation===
The MLIT's Aviation Bureau oversees 26 certified airports, including five international gateways: [[Okami International Airport]], [[Kansai International Airport (Okaiken)|Kansai International Airport]], [[Chūbu International Airport (Okaiken)|Chūbu International Airport]], [[Hakata International Airport]], and [[Sapporo International Airport (Okaiken)|Sapporo International Airport]]. Okami International Airport, which handled approximately 92 million passengers in 2025, is the busiest airport in Okaiken and the third-busiest in the [[East Fosian Sphere]].

The bureau administers bilateral air service agreements with over 50 countries and represents Okaiken in international aviation forums. The ongoing expansion of Okami International Airport's third terminal, scheduled for completion in 2028, is one of the MLIT's largest current capital projects, with a projected cost of {{currency|4.5 billion|SEN}}.

===Tourism===
{{Main|Tourism in Okaiken}}
Tourism is a significant and growing sector of the Sanese economy, with the [[Okaiken Tourism Agency]] reporting total tourism revenue of approximately {{currency|890 billion|SEN}} in FY 2025. The {{Nihongo|"Onsen Wellness Tourism"|温泉ウェルネスツーリズム|Onsen Werunesu Tsūrizumu}} programme is the flagship initiative, generating {{currency|210 billion|SEN}} annually and attracting 4.2 million international visitors to Okaiken's approximately 3,100 hot spring resorts. Key onsen destinations include [[Beppu]], [[Kusatsu]], [[Noboribetsu]], and [[Ureshino]].

The MLIT has identified tourism as a strategic growth sector, with the [[Okaiken Tourism Agency]] targeting 10 million international visitors annually by 2030. The agency's {{Nihongo|"Sustainable Tourism Strategy"|持続可能な観光戦略|Jizoku Kanō na Kankō Senryaku}}, published in 2024, emphasises the dispersal of tourism away from overcrowded urban centres toward rural and regional destinations, in line with the ministry's broader efforts to address the "Okami magnet" population concentration.

===Urban planning and the "Okami magnet"===
The {{Nihongo|"Okami magnet"|岡見磁器|Okami Jiki}} — the accelerating concentration of population, economic activity, and cultural capital in the Okami metropolitan area — is one of the MLIT's most pressing long-term challenges. Approximately 38% of Okaiken's 89.5 million population resides in the Okami metropolitan area, and the city accounts for approximately 45% of national GDP. This concentration places enormous strain on housing, transport, and social infrastructure, while contributing to depopulation and economic decline in rural areas.

The MLIT's City Bureau administers the [[Decentralised National Land Formation Plan]] ({{Nihongo|分散型国土形成計画|Bunsan-gata Kokudo Keisei Keikaku}}), first adopted in 2015 and revised in 2020, which seeks to promote regional development and reduce the Okami magnet effect through investment in regional infrastructure, digital connectivity, and the relocation of government functions. The plan's effectiveness has been debated, with critics noting that the Okami metropolitan area's share of national population has continued to increase despite the plan's stated objectives.

==Key legislation==

The MLIT administers and enforces numerous statutes governing transport, infrastructure, land use, and tourism. Key legislation includes:

* '''[[Railway Business Act (Okaiken)|Railway Business Act]]''' ({{Nihongo|鉄道事業法|Tetsudō Jigyō-hō}}, 1986, extensively amended 2026): The primary statute governing the licensing, operation, and regulation of rail operators in Okaiken. The 2026 amendment, enacted as part of the Two-Man Mandate legislation, requires all conventional rail services to operate with a minimum of two crew members and establishes the legal framework for the [[Yamamoto Hina Safety Commission]]'s grounding authority.
* '''[[Road Act (Okaiken)|Road Act]]''' ({{Nihongo|道路法|Dōro-hō}}, 1952): Governs the classification, construction, and maintenance of national and local roads. Establishes the hierarchy of expressways, national highways, prefectural roads, and municipal roads, and defines the responsibilities of each level of government for road management.
* '''[[Port Act (Okaiken)|Port Act]]''' ({{Nihongo|港湾法|Kōwan-hō}}, 1950): Regulates the development, management, and operation of ports and harbours. Establishes the classification of ports into specially designated ports, important ports, and local ports, and defines the roles of port authorities and the MLIT in port governance.
* '''[[Aviation Act (Okaiken)|Aviation Act]]''' ({{Nihongo|航空法|Kōkū-hō}}, 1952): Governs civil aviation, including the certification of airlines, the regulation of air navigation services, and the management of airports. Amended in 2019 to incorporate unmanned aerial vehicle (drone) regulation.
* '''[[National Land Use Planning Act (Okaiken)|National Land Use Planning Act]]''' ({{Nihongo|国土利用計画法|Kokudo Riyō Keikaku-hō}}, 1974): Establishes the framework for national and regional land-use planning, including the designation of urbanisation promotion areas, agricultural zones, and natural conservation areas.
* '''[[Two-Man Mandate Act (Okaiken)|Two-Man Mandate Act]]''' ({{Nihongo|二人乗務義務法|Futari Nōmu Gimu-hō}}, 2026): Requires all conventional rail services in Okaiken to operate with a minimum of two crew members. Enacted in direct response to the [[Nakayama Station dragging incident]], where one-man operation was identified as a contributing factor. The Act reversed the Honda cabinet's policy of expanding one-man operation to reduce operating costs.
* '''[[Tourism Nation Promotion Basic Act (Okaiken)|Tourism Nation Promotion Basic Act]]''' ({{Nihongo|観光立国推進基本法|Kankō Rikkoku Suishin Kihon-hō}}, 2007): Establishes the government's commitment to promoting tourism as a national strategic priority and defines the roles of the MLIT, the Okaiken Tourism Agency, and local governments in tourism development.

==Recent events==

===Nakayama Station dragging incident (March 2026)===
{{Main|Nakayama Station dragging incident}}
On 1 March 2026, 17-year-old [[Yamamoto Hina]] was killed at [[Nakayama Station]] when she was dragged by a departing train after the sole driver — operating under the one-man operation ({{Nihongo|ワンマン運転|Wanman Unten}}) system — failed to observe her boarding. The subsequent investigation by the [[Okaiken Transport Safety Board]] revealed that the driver had been distracted by a company-issued iPhone at the time of the incident, and that the one-man operation system — which eliminated the conductor's traditional role of verifying safe boarding — was a direct contributing factor.

The incident triggered the "[[March Maelstrom]]" — a wave of public protests, strikes, and civil unrest that ultimately led to the collapse of the [[Honda-Nakayama Cabinet]] and the [[Okaiken general election, 2026|2026 general election]]. The MLIT, under Minister [[Yasushi Kaneko]], was widely criticised for its failure to address safety concerns raised by the [[Okaiken Transport Workers Union|OTWU]] regarding the expansion of one-man operation, and for its perceived closeness to the rail operators who had lobbied for the policy.

===Yamamoto Hina Safety Commission (May 2026–present)===
{{Main|Yamamoto Hina Safety Commission}}
Established by executive order on 20 May 2026 — the first day of the [[Ikazaki Cabinet]] — the [[Yamamoto Hina Safety Commission]] is an independent rail safety body with the unprecedented authority to ground any rail service in Okaiken without ministerial approval if it determines that staffing levels or safety protocols are insufficient. The Commission is named after the victim of the Nakayama Station incident and represents the most significant reform to Okaiken's rail safety governance in the post-war era.

Transport Minister [[Seiran Mochizuki]], a former [[Okaiken Transport Workers Union|OTWU]] organiser, has direct oversight of the Commission's implementation. The Commission's independence from ministerial direction is enshrined in its founding executive order, which specifies that the Minister may not overrule a grounding decision. The Commission's first Chair, {{Nihongo|Noriko Shiomi|塩見法子|Shiomi Noriko}}, has stated that the Commission will conduct a comprehensive review of all one-man operation routes within its first year of operation.

===Two-Man Mandate implementation (May 2026–present)===
{{Main|Two-Man Mandate (Okaiken)}}
The {{Nihongo|'''Two-Man Mandate'''|二人乗務義務|Futari Nōmu Gimu}} — a core OSP demand during the coalition negotiations that formed the Ikazaki government — requires all conventional rail services in Okaiken to operate with a minimum of two crew members. The mandate was enacted through the [[Two-Man Mandate Act (Okaiken)|Two-Man Mandate Act]] and reversed the Honda cabinet's policy of expanding one-man operation to reduce operating costs.

Implementation oversight was assigned to Deputy Prime Minister [[Rento Nakayama]] in his capacity as Minister of Social Affairs, with Transport Minister Mochizuki responsible for operational enforcement. The mandate has been welcomed by the OTWU and rail safety advocates, but criticised by the Okaiken Railways Group enterprises and private operators, who have argued that the additional staffing costs — estimated at approximately {{currency|8.5 billion|SEN}} annually — will necessitate fare increases or service reductions on less profitable routes.

===Kizan prosecution (2026–present)===
{{Main|Kizan prosecution}}
The '''Kizan prosecution''' ({{Nihongo|木山起訴|Kiyama Kiso}}) is the first criminal case brought against a private infrastructure contractor in Okaiken for failures that resulted in loss of life. The prosecution targets {{Nihongo|Kizan Maintenance Corp.|木山メンテナンス|Kiyama Mentenansu}}, the private contractor responsible for track maintenance on the line where the Nakayama Station incident occurred, alleging that the company's systematic neglect of maintenance schedules and falsification of inspection records contributed to the unsafe conditions that led to Yamamoto Hina's death.

The case is being prosecuted by the [[Tokyo Special Prosecutors' Office (Okaiken)|Special Prosecutors' Office]] and is being closely watched as a test of the Sanese legal system's willingness to hold private contractors criminally liable for infrastructure failures. Kizan Maintenance Corp. has denied the charges, arguing that the primary cause of the incident was the one-man operation system, not maintenance failures.

==Controversies==

===One-man operation policy===
The expansion of one-man operation ({{Nihongo|ワンマン運転|Wanman Unten}}) on conventional rail lines was a signature policy of the Honda administration, promoted as a cost-saving measure that would improve the financial sustainability of rural and suburban rail services. The policy, which eliminated the conductor's role and left a single driver responsible for both operating the train and monitoring passenger boarding, was implemented over the strong objections of the [[Okaiken Transport Workers Union|OTWU]], which argued that it compromised safety.

The [[Nakayama Station dragging incident]] vindicated the OTWU's position and made the one-man operation policy a central political issue. The subsequent [[Two-Man Mandate]] reversed the policy, but the controversy has raised broader questions about the MLIT's regulatory culture and its relationship with the rail operators it oversees. Critics have argued that the ministry was too deferential to industry pressure and insufficiently responsive to safety concerns raised by workers and unions.

Minister Kaneko's handling of the one-man operation policy during his tenure was a particular focus of criticism. The OTWU had submitted formal safety concerns to the Railway Bureau in 2024 and 2025, but these were dismissed as "unsubstantiated" — a finding that was later contradicted by the Okaiken Transport Safety Board's investigation.

===Kizan prosecution and infrastructure privatisation===
The Kizan prosecution has intensified debate over the Honda administration's policy of outsourcing infrastructure maintenance to private contractors. Proponents of privatisation argue that it reduces costs and improves efficiency through competition; critics contend that it creates perverse incentives for contractors to cut corners on safety and maintenance to protect profit margins.

The case has also raised questions about the MLIT's oversight of private contractors. The [[Land Infrastructure Management and Construction Industry Agency (Okaiken)|Land Infrastructure Management Agency]], which is responsible for certifying and monitoring private contractors, has been criticised for its reliance on self-reported inspection data and its failure to conduct independent audits of contractor performance. The Ikazaki Cabinet has announced a comprehensive review of the contractor oversight system, with reforms expected to be proposed by the end of 2026.

===Aizawa Port Satori dependency===
The reliance on [[Satori AI|Satori]]-controlled automated cranes at [[Aizawa Port]] has been a subject of growing concern, particularly following the [[Satori AI Blackout]] of May 2026, which caused a complete halt of crane operations for 72 hours. The incident exposed the vulnerability of critical port infrastructure to AI system failures and prompted calls for the MLIT to mandate manual backup systems at all Satori-controlled facilities.

The MLIT has commissioned an independent review of AI dependency in critical port infrastructure, with findings expected by the end of 2026. Maritime Bureau officials have stated that the review will consider both the operational risks of AI dependency and the economic benefits of automated systems, and that its recommendations will inform future policy on the balance between automation and resilience.

===Public works spending and the "construction state"===
The MLIT's public works budget — approximately {{currency|128 billion|SEN}} in FY 2025, representing roughly 8% of total government expenditure — has long been a subject of political controversy. Critics argue that the legacy of the {{Nihongo|"construction state"|土建国家|Doken Kokka}} — the system of political patronage linking construction firms, bureaucrats, and rural politicians — continues to distort infrastructure spending toward unnecessary projects in rural constituencies at the expense of maintenance and renewal of existing assets in urban areas.

Supporters of public works spending argue that it serves important economic stabilisation and regional development functions, and that the reduction of the public works budget from its peak of approximately {{currency|200 billion|SEN}} in the late 1990s has already gone too far, contributing to the deterioration of Okaiken's ageing infrastructure stock. The [[Infrastructure Lifecycle Management Plan]], first published in 2016, projected that approximately {{currency|40 trillion|SEN}} in maintenance spending would be required over 30 years — a figure that many analysts consider insufficient given the accelerating deterioration of infrastructure built during the economic miracle era.

